A||||10||fryecar||Engine Light||||11||
Z||000000||fryecar||01-28-2000||12:09 PM||||Have had an error code for the last three years.... 35 IAC valve. I inspected the old one and found that it had been making good contact with the plenum, I ended up replacing it with same results.<BR>The light only comes on when I sit in traffic for a couple of minutes then goes out when I start to drive again after a few seconds.<BR>I've reset the codes by removing and replacing the positive battery cable also.||||reg||11
Z||000001||deceler8||01-28-2000||01:14 PM||||Having the same problem with a 2.5 liter engine...also replaced IAC and reset ECM...<P>Anyone ?||||reg||1
Z||000002||DRH||01-28-2000||06:02 PM||||The Online Service Guide (see link below) says code 35 is set if actual RPM is + or - 50 from the RPM the ECM is trying to set for 30 seconds or more. Besides the IAC it says to check for vacuum leaks. Does the car seem to be idling abnormally high or low when the light comes on?<P> <A HREF="http://www.mindspring.com/~fierosketch/osg/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.mindspring.com/~fierosketch/osg/</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ed||01-28-2000||07:10 PM||||Is your idle "hunting?" If it is I would second the motion on vacuum leaks. Check also the infamous pipe from your EGR valve to the intake manifold.  Breaks can be hidden by the insulator padding.  <P>Incorrect timing can cause improper idle speed too. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||01-29-2000||01:10 AM||||Another spot to check for a vacuum leak is the hard plastic vacuum lines that run under the top part of the intake plenum. I had one that was split and caused my idle to vary.<BR>The only way to inspect them is to follow where they go and replace it to see which is bad.||||reg||14
Z||000005||862M4inCA||01-29-2000||05:16 AM||||I too have had a problem with a code 35. Mostly while sitting at a stop light for longer than a minute, and noticed that when it came on I was idling at around 1100 RPM. I tried replacing it and about 2 months later was back to square one. The only way I've kept if from coming back on lately is to turn my blower fan (heater) on for a second and then back off and it seems to drop back to the 800-850 range. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Patrick||01-30-2000||03:32 PM||||<BR><B>DANGER, WILL ROBINSON, DANGER !!!!!</B><P>(You've got to be old to appreciate the quote above.)<P><B>fryecar</B>, never EVER remove the <B>positive</B> battery cable without first removing the <B>negative</B> cable. You're liable to cause a dead short while you've got a wrench on the positive terminal. <B>That could lead to the battery exploding in your face!!!</B><P>To clear the codes properly (or to do any electrical work safely), simply remove the <B>negative</B> battery cable.||||reg||4
Z||000007||Phaeton||01-30-2000||04:50 PM||||The more things change...new lost in space movie so young folks can relate somewhat.  Had a battery blow up in my face once (am very anal around batteries now) acid ate my hair, forehead, shirt, and luckily for me it destroyed my glasses which gave me enough time to get my eyes shut.  coworkers poured water on me while ripping clothes off, had to pull a piece of battery case out of my face.  Up to that point I was 'ya, sure' about batteries, now I even blow on the terminals as I take cables off to keep any possible fumes away.  Overkill? you bet.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Patrick||01-30-2000||04:57 PM||||<BR><B>Phaeton</B>, thanks for backing up my concerns about batteries exploding. It gives me the willies when I read posts like the one from <B>fryecar</B>.||||reg||14
Z||000009||fryecar||01-31-2000||12:18 PM||||Thanks for the concern guy's....  I guess I have a little maintenance bravery from time to time. If my old Aircraft Eletrician intructors from the NAVY Knew I did that they would kick my *ss.  ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Patrick||01-31-2000||03:03 PM||||<BR><B>fryecar</B>, you mean to say you knew better?<P><B>ARGHHHHHHHHH !!!!!</B>||||reg||4
